  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the time it takes to fill the entire tank. We know that the tank is being filled at 2 cubic feet per minute, except for the leakage caused by the hole. \n\nLet's first find the volume of the tank:\nVolume = π × r^2 × h = π × 4^2 × 10 = 160π cubic feet.\n\nWe have the tank filled to 5 feet after 10 minutes. So, the volume of water at that time is:\nVolume of water at 10 minutes = π × 4^2 × 5 = 80π cubic feet.\n\nNow, we need to find the leakage rate. Since the tank rises to 5 feet in 10 minutes, the volume it loses per minute is:\nVolume lost per minute = (80π - 200π) / 10 = 12π cubic feet per minute.\n\nThe leakage rate is proportional to the square root of the water's height. So, the leakage rate can be expressed as:\nLeakage rate = k × √(20 - t), where t is the time in minutes, and k is the constant of proportionality.\n\nSince the tank is filled at 2 cubic feet per minute, except for the leakage:\n2 cubic feet per minute = Leakage rate + 2 cubic feet per minute\n2 cubic feet per minute = k × √(20 - t) + 2 cubic feet per minute\n\nSimplifying, we get:\nk × √(20 - t) = 0\n\nBut since k is nonzero, it means that the tank is completely filled by the leakage, which is not possible. So, let's find the time it will take to fill the tank by the leakage alone.\n\nThe volume of the tank is 160π cubic feet. The leakage rate is 12π cubic feet per minute. So, the time to fill the tank by the leakage is:\nTime to fill the tank = Volume / Leakage rate = (160π) / (12π) = 40/3 minutes.\n\nNow, we need to find the time it takes to fill the entire tank using the leakage, which is 40/3 minutes.\n\nHowever, we need to find the overall time it takes to fill the entire tank. To do so, we need to find the time it takes to fill the tank using the leakage alone and the time it takes to fill the tank using the water supply alone (2 cubic feet per minute).\n\nSince the tank is initially empty, the time it takes to fill the tank using the water supply alone is:\nTime to fill the tank using water supply alone = Volume / Water supply rate = (160π) / 2 = 80π cubic feet / 2 = 40π cubic feet per minute.\n\nBut we don't have enough information to find the exact time it takes to fill the tank using the water supply alone, as the water level changes over time. So, we can't find the exact overall time it takes to fill the entire tank.\n\nHowever, we can find the time it takes to fill the tank using the leakage alone, which is 40/3 minutes. \n\nSo, the final answer is: \\boxed{40/3}",
